+\begin{proof}
+By Lemma~\ref{at:rv:split} and injectivity of $\rv$ on each finite fiber, $f$ decomposes into finitely many $t$-definable single-valued branches $g:\ga\fun t^\sharp$. A branch with $t=\infty$ is the constant zero branch. Any constant branch already has the required form, so fix a nonconstant branch, for which $t\in\RV$. Lemma~\ref{at:ball:t} makes $\ga$ atomic over $t$.
+
+Every coordinate line of $\ga$ is atomic over its fixed coordinates and $t$. By Lemma~\ref{atomic:open:ball:open}, its image under $g$ is a point or an open disc. Any two points of $\ga$ can be joined by changing one coordinate at a time. Starting at a point with image $p$ and ending at a point with different image, the first nonconstant line image is an open neighborhood of $p$ contained in $g(\ga)$. Thus $g(\ga)$ is open. It is a proper definable subset of $\VF$, since it lies in $t^\sharp$.
+
+By \cmin-minimality, every point of a proper definable open subset of $\VF$ lies in a maximal open disc contained in that subset. Choose such a disc $D\sub g(\ga)$ containing one value of $g$. An open line image that meets $D$ is contained in $D$: intersecting open discs are nested, and maximality excludes a larger one inside $g(\ga)$. A point image also stays in $D$ when its initial value is in $D$. The coordinate chains therefore keep every value of $g$ in $D$, so $g(\ga)=D$. The definable image of an atomic set is atomic over the same base, hence this open disc is $t$-atomic, as required.
+
 \end{proof}
 
